1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,410
An AI coding agent is a program
that reads the files of an app,

2
00:00:04,870 --> 00:00:05,690
plans a change,

3
00:00:06,370 --> 00:00:11,810
edits the code, and can run checks, all
from a request written in plain English.

4
00:00:12,730 --> 00:00:14,030
That sounds like magic,

5
00:00:14,530 --> 00:00:18,050
but it is closer to hiring a fast
helper who has never met you.

6
00:00:18,590 --> 00:00:22,710
It does exactly what it understood,
which is not always what you meant.

7
00:00:23,750 --> 00:00:25,330
Today you will direct one.

8
00:00:26,010 --> 00:00:29,370
Our app is a small,
fictional community resource finder.

9
00:00:30,170 --> 00:00:31,485
You will ask for one change,

10
00:00:32,110 --> 00:00:35,770
read what the agent changed,
test it, and report a problem.

11
00:00:36,810 --> 00:00:40,630
Then we look at what it would take to
turn this page into a real service.

12
00:00:45,543 --> 00:00:49,183
Before you ask for any change,
find out what works now.

13
00:00:50,723 --> 00:00:53,884
Open version 1 of the resource
finder and try a few searches.

14
00:00:55,043 --> 00:00:56,383
Library finds one resource.

15
00:00:57,243 --> 00:01:02,283
LIBRARY in capital letters also finds one,
because the page ignores capital letters.

16
00:01:03,663 --> 00:01:07,543
But learning finds nothing, even though
two resources are about learning.

17
00:01:08,623 --> 00:01:12,163
Version 1 only searches names
and descriptions, not categories.

18
00:01:13,523 --> 00:01:15,492
That is the one thing we want changed.

19
00:01:16,483 --> 00:01:20,763
Writing down what works today also
tells you what must not break tomorrow.

20
00:01:22,163 --> 00:01:23,663
Search version 1 for library,

21
00:01:24,323 --> 00:01:31,003
LIBRARY, learning, community,
and zzz, and write down each result.

22
00:01:32,063 --> 00:01:33,023
Pause the video now.

23
00:01:44,164 --> 00:01:45,844
A spec is a careful request.

24
00:01:46,824 --> 00:01:51,384
It names one change, who it helps,
and what must stay the same.

25
00:01:51,384 --> 00:01:53,164
Ours says:

26
00:01:54,244 --> 00:01:56,724
let the search also find
resources by category,

27
00:01:57,404 --> 00:02:00,504
for visitors who know the
kind of help but not the name.

28
00:02:01,804 --> 00:02:03,212
Keep capital letters matching,

29
00:02:03,624 --> 00:02:06,474
and keep the message that
appears when nothing matches.

30
00:02:07,524 --> 00:02:11,104
Then add acceptance checks,
tests anyone can run.

31
00:02:11,104 --> 00:02:14,804
I type learning, I expect two resources.

32
00:02:15,944 --> 00:02:18,124
I type LIBRARY, I expect one.

33
00:02:19,684 --> 00:02:23,904
Never put real names, passwords,
or secret keys in a request.

34
00:02:25,344 --> 00:02:28,090
Write your spec and three
checks a partner could run.

35
00:02:28,924 --> 00:02:29,863
Pause the video now.

36
00:02:45,065 --> 00:02:48,165
A good agent can show its plan
before it touches anything.

37
00:02:49,125 --> 00:02:53,485
Ask for that. Some agents can also
work on their own for a while,

38
00:02:53,905 --> 00:02:55,978
and you choose how much freedom to give.

39
00:02:56,405 --> 00:02:59,825
Either way, you review every
change before you accept it.

40
00:03:00,945 --> 00:03:04,905
Read the plan and make a prediction.
Which part of the page will change?

41
00:03:06,065 --> 00:03:07,051
About how many lines?

42
00:03:08,205 --> 00:03:12,185
What could break? Predicting first
makes you a sharper reviewer,

43
00:03:12,185 --> 00:03:14,025
because you know what to look for.

44
00:03:15,285 --> 00:03:16,318
Write your prediction:

45
00:03:16,985 --> 00:03:21,605
the part that will change, about how many
lines, and one thing that could break.

46
00:03:22,725 --> 00:03:23,865
Pause the video now.

47
00:03:35,048 --> 00:03:37,608
A diff shows exactly what changed.

48
00:03:38,788 --> 00:03:43,368
A line with a minus sign was removed,
and a line with a plus sign was added.

49
00:03:44,568 --> 00:03:48,288
Here, one added line puts
the category into the search.

50
00:03:49,168 --> 00:03:50,568
That is what we asked for.

51
00:03:51,968 --> 00:03:52,719
But look closer.

52
00:03:53,708 --> 00:03:59,288
Another line lost three words written
together in the code: to lower case.

53
00:04:00,328 --> 00:04:02,768
That is the step that
ignores capital letters.

54
00:04:03,668 --> 00:04:04,988
Nobody asked for that.

55
00:04:05,988 --> 00:04:07,428
You can check it yourself.

56
00:04:08,228 --> 00:04:11,328
Press Control and U to
see the page's code,

57
00:04:12,168 --> 00:04:16,648
then Control and F to
search it for to lower case.

58
00:04:17,828 --> 00:04:21,728
Version 1 has two matches,
and version 2 has one.

59
00:04:23,168 --> 00:04:25,448
Open the Mission 6B practice page,

60
00:04:25,908 --> 00:04:29,848
and mark each changed
line as asked for or not.

61
00:04:30,888 --> 00:04:31,928
Pause the video now.

62
00:04:46,096 --> 00:04:48,976
Now test the agent's version,
called version 2.

63
00:04:50,496 --> 00:04:53,836
Start with the happy path,
the normal use you expect to work.

64
00:04:54,656 --> 00:04:56,729
Type learning, and two resources appear.

65
00:04:57,896 --> 00:04:59,556
Type library, and one appears.

66
00:05:00,876 --> 00:05:03,736
Type zzz, and the no-match message shows.

67
00:05:05,436 --> 00:05:06,610
Then try capital letters.

68
00:05:07,336 --> 00:05:09,396
Type LIBRARY, and nothing is found.

69
00:05:10,356 --> 00:05:11,256
Version 1 found it.

70
00:05:12,176 --> 00:05:16,114
Something that used to work and broke
after a change is called a regression.

71
00:05:17,236 --> 00:05:19,956
Keep going,
because one failure does not end a test.

72
00:05:20,756 --> 00:05:23,761
Check that Tab moves a clear
outline through the controls,

73
00:05:24,116 --> 00:05:26,655
and that the page still
works in a narrow window.

74
00:05:27,576 --> 00:05:29,493
Run the five checks in your test log.

75
00:05:30,256 --> 00:05:31,296
Pause the video now.

76
00:05:42,396 --> 00:05:45,636
A bug report is a request
a stranger could follow.

77
00:05:46,356 --> 00:05:50,076
Give the steps:
open version 2 and type LIBRARY.

78
00:05:51,456 --> 00:05:55,496
Give what you expected:
one resource, as version 1 found.

79
00:05:56,596 --> 00:05:59,176
Give what actually happened: no matches.

80
00:06:00,696 --> 00:06:04,736
Then say what must stay the same:
keep the new category search.

81
00:06:05,716 --> 00:06:11,636
That last part matters, because a
careless fix can undo the work you wanted.

82
00:06:11,636 --> 00:06:14,796
Saying it is broken gives
the agent nothing to act on.

83
00:06:15,596 --> 00:06:19,056
A clear report usually gets a small,
careful repair.

84
00:06:20,176 --> 00:06:22,145
Write a repair request with the steps,

85
00:06:22,856 --> 00:06:26,096
what you expected,
what happened, and what to keep.

86
00:06:27,200 --> 00:06:28,139
Pause the video now.

87
00:06:39,089 --> 00:06:41,069
The agent sends back version 3.

88
00:06:41,749 --> 00:06:43,949
Do not trust it until you retest.

89
00:06:45,389 --> 00:06:47,202
First repeat the check that failed.

90
00:06:47,449 --> 00:06:50,349
Type LIBRARY,
and one resource appears again.

91
00:06:51,509 --> 00:06:52,429
Then repeat checks

92
00:06:52,429 --> 00:06:56,089
that passed before, because fixing
one thing can break another.

93
00:06:57,069 --> 00:07:01,489
Learning still finds two,
and zzz still shows the no-match message.

94
00:07:02,489 --> 00:07:03,329
Only when

95
00:07:03,329 --> 00:07:07,209
the failed check passes and nothing
else broke do you accept the repair.

96
00:07:08,229 --> 00:07:09,849
This loop is the whole job:

97
00:07:10,609 --> 00:07:15,729
ask clearly, read the change,
test it, report it, and retest.

98
00:07:17,169 --> 00:07:20,769
Run your checks on version 3 and
decide whether to accept the repair.

99
00:07:22,049 --> 00:07:23,189
Pause the video now.

100
00:07:37,598 --> 00:07:40,378
Our finder is one page
with fictional data.

101
00:07:41,378 --> 00:07:43,218
A real service needs much more.

102
00:07:44,218 --> 00:07:47,378
It needs an address,
a domain name you rent every year.

103
00:07:48,258 --> 00:07:50,018
It needs a home: hosting

104
00:07:50,018 --> 00:07:52,194
is a computer that keeps the site running,

105
00:07:52,358 --> 00:07:54,327
so anyone with the address can use it.

106
00:07:54,918 --> 00:07:56,918
To save favorites for many people,

107
00:07:57,358 --> 00:08:00,898
it needs a back end,
the part on a server that stores data.

108
00:08:00,898 --> 00:08:06,778
Sign-in must be checked on that server,
never by a picture of a sign-in box.

109
00:08:08,218 --> 00:08:09,678
A secret API key,

110
00:08:10,098 --> 00:08:14,278
the code that lets your app use a
paid service, stays on the server too.

111
00:08:15,358 --> 00:08:19,598
Plan the finder as a real service,
one roadblock at a time.

112
00:08:20,838 --> 00:08:21,777
Pause the video now.

113
00:08:36,177 --> 00:08:37,477
Here is what you practiced.

114
00:08:39,017 --> 00:08:40,379
You started from what worked,

115
00:08:40,777 --> 00:08:42,757
wrote one clear request with checks,

116
00:08:43,737 --> 00:08:44,723
predicted the change,

117
00:08:45,557 --> 00:08:51,457
read the diff, tested, reported a
regression, and retested the repair.

118
00:08:52,917 --> 00:08:56,337
That loop works whether the
helper is an AI agent or a person.

119
00:08:57,237 --> 00:08:59,206
And someone must still own the result.

120
00:08:59,837 --> 00:09:02,531
A real service needs a
caretaker who pays the bills,

121
00:09:02,697 --> 00:09:03,495
keeps it updated,

122
00:09:03,937 --> 00:09:08,577
backs up the data, helps users, and
reviews every change before it goes live.

123
00:09:09,557 --> 00:09:11,215
An agent can write code quickly.

124
00:09:11,777 --> 00:09:13,185
It cannot take responsibility.

125
00:09:14,157 --> 00:09:15,617
That part stays with the people

126
00:09:15,617 --> 00:09:16,556
who run the service.
